            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house is already VERY air tight, and no further air leakage reduction should be undertaken until you add more mechanical ventilation to this house - see the Ventilation System section of this report.



In conjunction with installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system, with a minimum 75% efficiency (designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI), you can also address the following air leakage locations:



** Around the attic access panel (can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



** Many of the original wood windows( especially the 2nd floor) that are rotting out due to excess condensation &amp; mould.



** The chimney pipe &amp; the combustion air supply pipe for the previous wood stove (now disconnected) need to be completely sealed.



Savings: There is about $85 of estimated annual savings from completing the above air sealing work. As you will see in the Ventilation System section of my report, there is an estimated $140 per year to provide adequate mechanical ventilation by the new HRV. The net annual cost savings of about $55 is a great investment to protect the indoor air quality of your home &amp; ensure your new replacement windows do not rot away also.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

Many of the original wood windows in this house (especially the ones on the 2nd floor) are suffering the damage associated with inadequate house ventilation ......... condensation &amp; mould buildup that has resulted in rotting of the frame and now air leakage as they no longer seal well. The 2nd floor wood windows, as well as the basement aluminum windows are the ones in the highest need of replacement, although you will likely get the best deal (per window) if you just upgrade all of the wood windows at once.



I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of the current windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing the windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 499 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 6.9 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</windows>
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem>

Please ensure you read the Heating System section of this report before reading this section.



This house was originally built to be very air tight, and because there was no heating/cooling air distribution from a ducted system (eg. a furnace), this house relies on mechanical ventilation to manage indoor air quality, including the removal of excess humidity that causes condensation, mould and rotting of the windows. Most new high efficiency housing from the 80's &amp; 90's had early generation Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) systems installed to manage ventilation throughout the house, including an adequate fresh air supply to bedrooms &amp; living areas, as well as exhaust ventilation from bathrooms &amp; living/kitchen areas. Your house had an HRV installed, however it has been fully disconnected and appears to have suffered a failure (part of the housing looks melted).



Other than the HRV, there is no mechanical exhaust (kitchen range hood fan, bathroom fans), and there is no fresh air supply system. The only mechanical exhaust are the 2 x clothes dryers, and it is GREAT that this moisture is going to the outside.



The impact of almost no ventilation is an excess buildup of moisture &amp; condensation inside the house for many years ........... resulting in the mould buildup and rotting of the original wood windows - very unfortunate.



The original HRV appears to have a very "creative" ductwork system that goes up through the main floor closet and into the pantry/storage area. There also appears to be exhaust air ducts in both of the bathrooms. I am not totally sure how the ductwork flows for that original HRV, however it needs to be investigated further by a certified HRV designer/installer.



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system, with a minimum 75% efficiency, designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



Savings: By installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, with a minimum 75% efficiency, there is an estimated increase of $ 140 per year in energy costs, however this will likely be offset by about $85 of annual savings from current "uncontrolled" air leakage - see the Air Sealing section of my report. The net additional cost for this required ventilation is only about $55 per year. This is the best investment ever, as it will protect your indoor air quality and reduce/eliminate the condensation, mold &amp; rotting of the next set of windows !



There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

I have modeled a combination heating &amp; hot water system for this house - please see the recommendation in the Heating System section of this report.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

You are a new owner to this house and it is your first winter heating season. From our discussion, it sounds like the original owner of this 1981 house had it constructed to high efficiency building standards so that it could be heated primarily by electric baseboard heaters on all 3 floors, including the basement.



The house was also designed with a wood stove heating system in the basement, however there is no way to distribute that heat other than holes cut in the ceiling &amp; floors of the basement, main floor and second floor. As you have some guests living in the basement of your house, you are trying to allow privacy by closing off these holes. In addition, you found out when purchasing the house that the wood stove did not comply to current safety code regulations so it is disconnected and is no longer used. NOTE: this wood stove was also being used to heat the domestic hot water for this house ........ during the heating season.



As the cost of electricity has increased by about 50% in the past 10 years (I'm not even sure how much it was back in the 80's), the cost of using "all electric" heating was likely becoming significant for the previous owner, so they started using the wood stove more as a primary heating system, In your situation, you cannot do this.



There is one more very important factor to consider - this house has no central ventilation system (ie: a Heat Recovery Ventilator - HRV) to manage indoor air quality or ductwork system to manage heat flow between floors - please see the Ventilation section of this report. The consequences have been significant in both poor indoor air quality &amp; excess humidity &amp; condensation on the wood windows, causing them to have mould &amp; rotting issues.



I think you need a simpler system that is cost-effective and helps to improve indoor air quality. I am thinking outside the box with the following:

1. Install a 95% propane combination heating &amp; hot water boiler.

2. Install a new 75% efficient Energy Star HRV - see Ventilation section of this report



For the propane combination boiler, it may fit into the small room where the electric hot water tank is currently located. To distribute the heating pipes from that boiler to the main &amp; second floors, I suspect the contractor will need to remove a section of drywall around the perimeter of the basement &amp; main floor ceilings (not really a huge issue &amp; easy enough to fix up afterwards). For the basement heating, I think they can use the same basement ceiling opening to run the pipes around to where they need the heat &amp; then down to the basement floor. 



Savings: By installing a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas "combi boiler" (providing both heat &amp; domestic hot water), you could potentially save $ 4,637 per year in energy costs ($3,453 in heating &amp; $1,184 in hot water), increase your annual energy usage by 2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. These savings include the "net" of electricity costs saved less the additional cost of the propane used.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two toilets that are older units that use a lot of water &amp; should be replaced with a lower water consumption models. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (just the 2 x clothes dryers, as there is no kitchen range hood or bathroom exhaust fans and the HRV has been disconnected), indicated a pressure drop of 9.3 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The only appliance that was chimney vented (and susceptible to the back drafting of carbon monoxide) was the wood stove, and you have had it disconnected &amp; no longer use it ......... as it was not code compliant.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke detectors however did not see any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Smith



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.26 per kWh

Oil  - $0.90 per litre 

Propane - $0.73

Wood - $262.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.46 per bag, $299.62 per tonne bulk delivered



